OVERVIEW:

 This program reads magic squares from files in a particular
 format and determines their validity as a magic square. It
 can also write magic squares in this same format using an
 algorithm which uses a given odd-number dimension and file
 path to write the square to.


INCLUDED FILES:

 * MagicSquare.java - mandatory class file
 * MagicSquareDriver.java - mandatory driver file
 * MagicSquareInterface.java - mandatory interface file for MagicSquare class
 * README - this file


COMPILING AND RUNNING:

 There are no subdirectories present nor needed for this
 program to run. Only this program's driver class needs to
 be compiled; all other source files will be compiled
 automatically. In order to compile it successfully, the
 command-line's working directory should be the same as
 the program's directory, then execute the following command:
 $ javac MagicSquareDriver.java

 In order for the program to exit successfully, it first
 needs a valid combination of arguments for validating and
 generating squares. In order to validate squares, execute
 the command
 $ java MagicSquareDriver -check <filename>
 ... where filename is a valid file path with a correctly
 formatted pre-existing magic square.
 
 In order to generate squares, execute the command
 $ java MagicSquareDriver -create <filename> <size>
 ... where filename is the desired write path, which is not
 required to exist prior to execution, and size is an odd
 integer that is at least three (3) which is necessary for
 the algorithm to generate squares.

 The console which the program is executed in will print the
 results of both validating and generating squares.


PROGRAM DESIGN AND IMPORTANT CONCEPTS:

 The MagicSquareInterface was provided by the instructions,
 and was used to create the MagicSquare class. Interfaces
 cannot declare nor provide constructors, so its primary
 use in this assignment was mandating three (3) methods
 that would be implemented in the MagicSquare class.

 The MagicSquare class ended up being the primary provider
 of all necessary code, which contains 268 readable lines.
 It handles practically everything from reading and writing
 files, validating and generating squares, all while
 managing the instance variables for each MagicSquare object.
 It implements the aforementioned interface, so it provides
 functionality for its abstract methods: isMagicSquare(),
 getMatrix(), and toString().

 In order to handle bad input, the program prints an error
 specifying the problem and prints the usage statements. Bad
 input could mean a variety of things, but in this case it
 primarily means a lack of arguments, bad flags, or the
 specified size being invalid. It also means the program will
 detect and exit if the specified filename from the command-
 line cannot be read or written to. To my knowledge, there are
 no edge cases where a possible error isn't handled manually,
 or the program will exit unexpectedly and print a stack trace.

 During the validation phase if it is chosen, it walks through
 a couple checks to ensure it's even a square to begin with.
 It first confirms every row is the right length, then checks
 that each value is only seen once with a boolean[] array sized
 to the dimension squared plus one (1). This array is indexed
 by value to represent each possible value; if a value has
 already been marked as seen, then it is automatically not a
 magic square. The rest of the phase is fairly straightforward,
 as it checks that each row, column, and possible diagonal all
 sum up to the magic number.

 The generation phase uses what I learned is a "Siamese-method
 placement algorithm." There is a small caveat though, in that
 it does not begin in the top-middle square as a typical Siamese
 square would; it instead begins in the bottom-middle square.
 The steps are also similar but not identical: instead of
 advancing a square to the top-right, it advances squares to the
 bottom-right. The process is otherwise the same, wrapping around
 row or column to the opposite edge if it falls off the matrix.
 If the next square is already filled, it moves up one row from
 the previous square.

 If anyone is to redesign this, I would reconsider if the
 inclusion of the interface is entirely necessary, unless you're
 future-proofing for a later project. The MagicSquare class is
 also handling four (4) unrelated tasks (namely reading, writing,
 validating, and generating squares), but the interface's required
 constructors more or less forced that structure.



TESTING:

 In order to test my program, the MagicSquareTest driver,
 valid squares, and invalid squares were used. I also
 generated 7 x 7 and 9 x 9 squares and used both the program
 and manual validation (as in, checking each row, column,
 and diagonal all equaled the "magic number") to ensure the
 output was correct.

 This program is certainly programmer-proof, but you can't
 trust programs which proclaim to be idiot-proof. You can only
 misuse something so hard, but the limits to that misuse is
 hard to gauge. This is about as good as it gets in terms of
 idiot-proofing, and I know this because I tested a very wide
 variety of possible argument combinations and errors, and
 caught all I was instructed to catch and all that were not
 mentioned in the project description.

 Currently, there is *technically* only one (1) known "issue,"
 and it involves how the rows in matrixArray are iterated through
 in the toString() method, which uses a simple for loop instead
 of a collection loop. This is only a technical problem because
 it means the program will run microseconds slower, and I could
 not care less about optimizing a command-line program if its
 functionality is not harmed by its current state of being. This
 "optimization" is also not only unnecessary but risks
 sacrificing readability.


DISCUSSION:

 One of the trickiest bugs actually involved my driver class
 rather than the MagicSquare class, more particularly handling
 command-line arguments. My first instinct in creating the file
 was to organize the program such that my variable declarations
 were at the top for readability. However, when beginning my
 first tests, it often crashed with a stack trace instead of a
 known error. This was because I was assigning the values of
 these variables before I confirmed that there were enough
 arguments to begin with, sometimes leading me to try to assign
 a completely non-existent value to a variable, throwing an
 ArrayIndexOutOfBounds exception. Perhaps this is a rookie
 mistake, but to me it was a valuable one. The fix was to break
 up my argument handling (between checking args.length and the
 flags) so that it isn't possible for the program to continue
 without enough arguments.

 I also noticed early on in testing that not every magic square
 file was formatted the same. From first impressions, I thought
 each file used spaces to separate each value. However, some
 files use tab characters, causing the program to throw an error
 stating that the file was not in an expected format (which was
 true!). This forced me to rework my understanding of how the
 files were formatted and how to handle each, which the Java
 documentation thankfully helped answer: when scanning between
 each value, there is a special RegEx combination (\\s+) which,
 when used with the value Scanner object's useDelimiter() method,
 can handle all whitespace until the next value.

 I will say that by the end of this assignment, the only segment
 of code I didn't understand (until further research, that is) was
 the algorithm for generating magic squares. I could trace by hand
 why each step made sense to fill each square on a small scale, but
 that became harder with larger and larger squares.

